==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From Greek {{term|ὑπό|sc=polytonic|tr=hypo||under}} + {{term|φύσις|sc=polytonic|tr=physis||nature}} from {{term|φύω|sc=polytonic|tr=phyō||to bring forth}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pl=hypophyses}}

# {{anatomy}} The [[pituitary gland]].
#* '''1915''' - Wilhelm Falta, (trans. Milton Kayton Meyers) ''[http://books.google.com/books?id=sGEPAAAAYAAJ The Ductless Glandular Diseases]'', P. Blakiston's Son & Co. [http://worldcat.org/wcpa/oclc/4634141 OCLC 4634141], page 231
#*: CHAPTER VI / THE DISEASES OF THE '''HYPOPHYSIS''' <nowiki>[PITUITARY GLAND]</nowiki> <!-- Square brackets in original-->
# {{botany}} The top cell of the suspensor in a dicot embryo, which will differentiate to form part of the root cap.
#* '''1995''' - Trevor A. Thorpe, ''In Vitro Embryogenesis in Plants'', Springer, ISBN 0792331494, page 85
#*: All the cells of the suspensor, except one, are eventually crushed and obliterated by the growing embryo; this fortunate cell known as the '''hypophysis''' is the one in immediate contact with the embryo and plays an important role in the formation of the root apex.

